<td id="S3.T5.6.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T5.6.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8</span></td>
<td id="S3.T5.6.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><math id="S3.T5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\!=\!\max(0.4\!-\!0.05(I_{m}\!-\!1),0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">λ</mi><mo lspace="0.148em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.148em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.800em">0.05</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\!=\!\max(0.4\!-\!0.05(I_{m}\!-\!1),0)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T5.6.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T5.6.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">79.12</span></td>
<td id="S3.T5.6.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S3.T5.6.10.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">79.45</span></td>
<td id="S3.T5.6.10.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T5.6.10.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">26.33</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">D </span>Dimension Mapping for Parameter Pruning</h2>

<div id="S4a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.F2" title="In TextVQA ‣ B Detailed Description of Datasets and Evaluation Metrics ‣ Collaborative Multi-Mode Pruning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> illustrates the detailed dimension mapping process in parameter pruning. In the FFN module, all operations follow the standard per-channel definition described in <a href="#S3" title="3 Methodology ‣ Collaborative Multi-Mode Pruning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
For the MHA module, pruning is performed in a head-wise manner, <em id="S4a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="S4a.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span>, all channels within a head are pruned simultaneously to preserve parallelism after compression. Taking the <math id="S4a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-th head as an example, this requires that the mask values within the head remain identical, which can be formally expressed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{M}^{p}_{(h-1)d_{k}+1}=\bm{M}^{p}_{(h-1)d_{k}+2}=...=\bm{M}^{p}_{hd_{k}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑴</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑴</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑴</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{M}^{p}_{(h-1)d_{k}+1}=\bm{M}^{p}_{(h-1)d_{k}+2}=...=\bm{M}^{p}_{hd_{k}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4a.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Meanwhile, the prunable units are reduced from <math id="S4a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> channels to <math id="S4a.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> heads. For each unit, its importance score is calculated as the average of the channel-level importance scores within the corresponding head:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{S}^{\prime p}_{h}=\frac{1}{d_{k}}\sum_{i=(h-1)d_{k}+1}^{hd_{k}}\bm{S}^{\prime p}_{i,:}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑺</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mi>𝑺</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{S}^{\prime p}_{h}=\frac{1}{d_{k}}\sum_{i=(h-1)d_{k}+1}^{hd_{k}}\bm{S}^{\prime p}_{i,:}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4a.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, in our CIM module, the mask <math id="S4a.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{M}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑴</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{M}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is transferred from the channel dimension to the attention head dimension, resulting in <math id="S4a.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{M}}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{M}}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Self-corrected Token Importance Metric ‣ 3.2 Collaborative Importance Metric ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Collaborative Multi-Mode Pruning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, where <math id="S4a.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{M}}^{p}_{h}=\bm{M}^{p}_{hd_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑴</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{M}}^{p}_{h}=\bm{M}^{p}_{hd_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">E </span>More Experimental Results and Analysis</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Real-World Inference Latency</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CoMP employs a structured pruning scheme, which is widely recognized for its ease of model deployment without requiring hardware-specific adaptations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>.
To demonstrate the real-world efficiency of CoMP, we report the inference latency and speedup ratio on a single RTX 4090 GPU in <a href="#S2.T3" title="In TextVQA ‣ B Detailed Description of Datasets and Evaluation Metrics ‣ Collaborative Multi-Mode Pruning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. On BLIP-NLVR2 with a FLOPs pruning ratio of 0.85, CoMP achieves a 3.85<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> speedup while maintaining state-of-the-art accuracy, as reported in <a href="#S4.T1" title="In 4 Experimental Results and Analysis ‣ Collaborative Multi-Mode Pruning for Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table F</span>: </span><span id="S5.T6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation results of the interval steps <math id="S5.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> on BLIP using the NLVR2 dataset at a pruning ratio of 0.8. The default setting is <span id="S5.T6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T6.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
